I do remember was actually

00:05:23.350 --> 00:05:25.090
Steve Kroon and Herman.

00:05:25.090 --> 00:05:27.940
Herman is also a colleague
who organized this.

00:05:27.940 --> 00:05:31.750
And this poster will be at
NIPS, this year's NIPS poster.

00:05:31.750 --> 00:05:33.900
And they have a
closure, they have

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:37.580
discovered about how to
add noise in a network.

00:05:37.580 --> 00:05:40.570
So how much noise can
you add in a network

00:05:40.570 --> 00:05:42.700
until you can't learn any more?

00:05:42.700 --> 00:05:44.440
So actually, you
saturate the network,

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:46.840
and you cannot anymore
learn in a deep net.

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:49.180
And so they have these
closure properties

00:05:49.180 --> 00:05:52.660
about how deep you can go, and
how much noise you can add,

00:05:52.660 --> 00:05:54.970
and that's really
beautiful because so much--

00:05:54.970 --> 00:05:59.214
I mean, that just narrows the
parameter tuning search space.

00:05:59.214 --> 00:06:01.630
This is work that's being done
right here in Stellenbosch.

00:06:01.630 --> 00:06:02.620
MELANIE WARRICK: That's great.

